WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.640
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.071
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.371 --> 00:00:16.842
Unit fifty-two: The Sharing Economy. In this unit, you will discuss platforms, sharing and the future of ownership.

4
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.107
sharing economy.

5
00:00:19.807 --> 00:00:24.616
sharing economy means an economic system where people share or rent assets.

6
00:00:24.916 --> 00:00:29.040
The sharing economy includes car sharing and home rentals.

7
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:33.437
Your turn. Say: sharing economy.

8
00:00:36.137 --> 00:00:37.823
sharing economy.

9
00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:40.247
platform economy.

10
00:00:40.947 --> 00:00:46.325
platform economy means business activity organised through digital platforms.

11
00:00:46.625 --> 00:00:49.322
Delivery apps are part of the platform economy.

12
00:00:50.022 --> 00:00:53.185
Your turn. Say: platform economy.

13
00:00:55.885 --> 00:00:57.315
platform economy.

14
00:00:58.317 --> 00:00:59.527
peer-to-peer.

15
00:01:00.227 --> 00:01:04.618
peer-to-peer means directly between individuals, without a company in the middle.

16
00:01:04.918 --> 00:01:07.962
Peer-to-peer lending connects borrowers and lenders.

17
00:01:08.662 --> 00:01:11.280
Your turn. Say: peer-to-peer.

18
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:14.969
peer-to-peer.

19
00:01:15.983 --> 00:01:17.170
gig platform.

20
00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:21.414
gig platform means an app that offers short-term jobs.

21
00:01:21.714 --> 00:01:24.387
She finds design work through a gig platform.

22
00:01:25.087 --> 00:01:28.215
Your turn. Say: gig platform.

23
00:01:30.915 --> 00:01:32.021
gig platform.

24
00:01:33.017 --> 00:01:34.262
asset sharing.

25
00:01:34.962 --> 00:01:39.434
asset sharing means using items together instead of each person owning one.

26
00:01:39.734 --> 00:01:41.838
Asset sharing reduces waste.

27
00:01:42.538 --> 00:01:45.632
Your turn. Say: asset sharing.

28
00:01:48.332 --> 00:01:49.646
asset sharing.

29
00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:51.663
trust rating.

30
00:01:52.363 --> 00:01:56.290
trust rating means a score showing how reliable a user is.

31
00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:59.936
Drivers with low trust ratings may lose their accounts.

32
00:02:00.636 --> 00:02:03.880
Your turn. Say: trust rating.

33
00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:07.674
trust rating.

34
00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:10.044
platform worker.

35
00:02:10.744 --> 00:02:14.845
platform worker means a person who earns money through digital platforms.

36
00:02:15.145 --> 00:02:17.586
Platform workers often lack sick pay.

37
00:02:18.286 --> 00:02:21.275
Your turn. Say: platform worker.

38
00:02:23.975 --> 00:02:25.220
platform worker.

39
00:02:26.217 --> 00:02:27.856
collaborative consumption.

40
00:02:28.556 --> 00:02:33.876
collaborative consumption means sharing, swapping or renting goods instead of buying them.

41
00:02:34.176 --> 00:02:37.348
Clothes-swapping events encourage collaborative consumption.

42
00:02:38.048 --> 00:02:41.513
Your turn. Say: collaborative consumption.

43
00:02:44.213 --> 00:02:45.818
collaborative consumption.

44
00:02:46.817 --> 00:02:49.048
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:49.348 --> 00:02:51.928
Do you use ride-hailing apps, Ms. Sarah?

46
00:02:53.217 --> 00:02:55.045
Every day. Do you?

47
00:02:56.350 --> 00:03:01.257
Sometimes. My brother works on one, actually. He's a platform worker.

48
00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:03.911
Does he enjoy it?

49
00:03:05.217 --> 00:03:10.272
He says he does, mostly. The hours are flexible, but the pay isn't stable.

50
00:03:11.583 --> 00:03:15.884
Many platform workers say the same. Some have no insurance at all.

51
00:03:17.183 --> 00:03:22.262
His trust rating matters enormously. One bad review, and he loses income.

52
00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:28.487
The sharing economy offers freedom, but security too often doesn't come with it.

53
00:03:29.783 --> 00:03:34.534
Grammar focus: Ellipsis and substitution (mastery).

54
00:03:34.834 --> 00:03:37.449
Fluent speakers use ellipsis constantly.

55
00:03:37.749 --> 00:03:41.194
Every day. Do you? He says he does, mostly.

56
00:03:41.494 --> 00:03:43.575
Many platform workers say the same.

57
00:03:43.875 --> 00:03:52.202
Native-like fluency depends on ellipsis: repeating full sentences sounds unnatural in conversation.

58
00:03:53.217 --> 00:03:56.391
Now read and listen. Access Over Ownership.

59
00:03:56.691 --> 00:04:01.466
The sharing economy has changed how millions of people travel, eat and work.

60
00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:07.478
Instead of owning cars or tools, consumers can access them when needed.

61
00:04:08.783 --> 00:04:14.672
Peer-to-peer platforms connect individuals directly, often across cities and countries.

62
00:04:15.983 --> 00:04:20.723
Supporters say asset sharing reduces waste and makes services cheaper.

63
00:04:22.017 --> 00:04:25.827
Critics say the same platforms can leave workers without protection.

64
00:04:27.117 --> 00:04:32.239
Platform workers usually depend on trust ratings, which can be harsh and unpredictable.

65
00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:38.220
Some governments now require gig platforms to provide insurance; others don't.

66
00:04:39.517 --> 00:04:44.349
The future of work may depend on how fairly the platform economy treats its workers.

67
00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:48.546
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:48.846 --> 00:04:53.504
Directly between individuals, without a company in the middle, is blank.

69
00:04:53.804 --> 00:04:58.561
A, peer-to-peer. B, platform economy. C, asset sharing.

70
00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:05.084
The answer is A: peer-to-peer.

71
00:05:06.583 --> 00:05:09.343
'I love this app.' 'So blank I.'

72
00:05:09.643 --> 00:05:12.669
A, am. B, do. C, have.

73
00:05:16.683 --> 00:05:19.252
The answer is B: do.

74
00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:24.270
Some countries regulate platforms; others blank.

75
00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:28.851
A, don't. B, doesn't. C, aren't.

76
00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.291
The answer is A: don't.

77
00:05:36.783 --> 00:05:40.176
A score showing how reliable a user is, is a blank.

78
00:05:40.476 --> 00:05:45.778
A, trust rating. B, gig platform. C, collaborative consumption.

79
00:05:49.783 --> 00:05:52.387
The answer is A: trust rating.

80
00:05:53.883 --> 00:05:57.450
According to the text, what do some governments now require?

81
00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:03.331
A, higher prices. B, insurance for platform workers. C, fewer drivers.

82
00:06:07.317 --> 00:06:10.617
The answer is B: insurance for platform workers.

83
00:06:12.117 --> 00:06:17.091
Now it's your turn. Part one: Is the sharing economy good for workers?

84
00:06:22.391 --> 00:06:29.441
Part two: Use ellipsis or substitution twice, give one benefit, one problem and a solution.

85
00:06:35.741 --> 00:06:49.219
Partly. It offers flexible hours; traditional jobs often don't. The problem is security: many workers have none. A solution would be basic insurance, and some countries already require it.

86
00:06:49.519 --> 00:06:52.598
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:53.583 --> 00:06:58.976
Great work today! You learned eight new words and ellipsis and substitution (mastery).

88
00:06:59.276 --> 00:07:02.671
Today's value is respect. Thank the people who help us.

89
00:07:02.971 --> 00:07:06.947
See you in Unit fifty-three. Goodbye!

90
00:07:07.247 --> 00:07:09.934
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

